+# Marginalia Books
+
+A static website for Marginalia Books, a fictional independent bookshop on
+Quill Street in Lowmoor. It has four pages: home, books, events and visit.
+The books page has a genre filter whose state lives in the URL, the events page
+lists upcoming readings and story times, and the visit page has opening hours,
+the address, getting-here notes and a contact form. The contact form is a demo:
+it checks the fields in the browser and shows a notice, but it never sends
+anything anywhere.
+
+The site is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no build step, no frameworks
+and no runtime dependencies. It makes no requests outside its own origin: no
+CDNs, web fonts, remote images or analytics.
+
+## Viewing the site
+
+Serve the repo root with any static server and open it in a browser. From the
+repo root, either of these works:
+
+```sh
+npx http-server -p 8080 -s .
+# or
+python3 -m http.server 8080
+```
+
+Then open http://localhost:8080/. Opening `index.html` directly from the file
+system also works for browsing, but use a server for the most accurate check.
+
+## File layout
+
+```
+index.html          Home page
+books.html          Books catalogue with genre filter
+events.html         Events listing
+visit.html          Opening hours, address, access and contact form
+css/styles.css      Shared styles for every page
+js/catalog.js       Catalogue data, prices in pounds (add or change books here only)
+js/books.js         Genre filter and URL state for the books page
+js/home.js          Staff picks and today's opening hours on the home page
+js/contact.js       Contact form validation and demo submit message
+favicon.svg         Site icon
+tests/              Headless browser tests (Node test runner + Playwright)
+```
+
+Opening hours appear in two places: the table on `visit.html` and the `HOURS`
+list in `js/home.js`, which drives the "today" line on the home page. Change
+both together.
+
+## Running the tests
+
+The tests start their own static server on a random port and drive headless
+Chrome through Playwright. Playwright is expected to be installed globally,
+with google-chrome available at `/usr/bin/google-chrome`. If the Chrome channel
+is not available, the tests fall back to Playwright's bundled Chromium.
+
+```sh
+npm test
+```
+
+To run one file:
+
+```sh
+NODE_PATH=$(npm root -g) node --test tests/books.test.js
+```
+
+`npm install` also works, because Playwright is listed under `devDependencies`.
+The tests resolve it from the local `node_modules` first, then from the global
+install.
